<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec3-overdamped-never-stretched" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing an Overdamped System: Is the Spring Ever Stretched?</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO: STATE THE GUIDING QUESTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In <xref ref="sec-overdamped-analysis"/> we used the sign of <m>x(0)</m>, the sign
    of <m>x'(0)</m>, and whether the mass crosses equilibrium to build a complete
    picture of the motion. In this section, we put that same approach to work on a
    new question: for the system below, is the spring <alert>ever stretched</alert>?
    We'll work through an activity to build up the picture, then come back to this
    question at the end.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SUMMARY ASSEMBLAGE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We continue analyzing overdamped and critically damped systems.
    Recall the shared behavior of these two cases.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-overdamped-never-stretched-summary">
    <title>Summary: Overdamped and Critically Damped Cases of <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m></title>
    <tabular halign="left" row-headers="yes">
      <col width="22%"/>
      <col width="36%"/>
      <col width="20%"/>
      <col width="22%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Discriminant and roots</cell>
        <cell>Crossings of <m>x=0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Overdamped</term> (larger <m>c</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 > 4km</m> (two distinct real roots)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Critically damped</term> (borderline)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 = 4km</m> (one repeated real root)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SETUP: ODE AND SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Consider the spring-mass-dashpot system with <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 3</m>,
    <m>k = 2</m>, set in motion at <m>t = 0</m> with <m>x(0) = 0</m> and
    <m>x'(0) = -2</m>. Substituting these parameters gives the IVP:
    <me>x'' + 3x' + 2x = 0, \quad x(0) = 0, \quad x'(0) = -2.</me>
    The solution to this IVP is:
    <me>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}.</me>
  </p>

  <solution>
    <title>Work: Solution to the IVP (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      With <m>m=1</m>, <m>c=3</m>, <m>k=2</m> the characteristic equation is
      <m>r^2 + 3r + 2 = 0</m>, which factors as <m>(r+1)(r+2) = 0</m>.
      This gives two distinct real roots <m>r_1 = -1</m> and <m>r_2 = -2</m>,
      confirming the system is overdamped. The general solution is:
      <me>x(t) = c_1 e^{-t} + c_2 e^{-2t}.</me>
      Applying <m>x(0) = 0</m>:
      <me>c_1 + c_2 = 0 \implies c_2 = -c_1.</me>
      Differentiating:
      <me>x'(t) = -c_1 e^{-t} - 2c_2 e^{-2t}.</me>
      Applying <m>x'(0) = -2</m>:
      <me>-c_1 - 2c_2 = -2 \implies -c_1 + 2c_1 = -2 \implies c_1 = -2.</me>
      So <m>c_1 = -2</m>, <m>c_2 = 2</m>, and <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FRAMING: GOAL OF THE ACTIVITY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    With that question in mind, let's work through the following activity to
    map out the behavior of the system.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: BUILDING THE PICTURE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="activity-overdamped-never-stretched">
    <title>Building a Picture of the Motion</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Throughout this activity, <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task 1: Which damping case -->

    <task xml:id="act-never-stretched-which-case">
      <title>Which Damping Case?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Based on the form of <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m>,
          which damping case are we in?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Overdamped</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The solution is a sum of two distinct decaying exponentials
              with roots <m>r_1 = -1</m> and <m>r_2 = -2</m>. Two distinct real
              roots means <m>c^2 > 4km</m>, which is the overdamped case.
              <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Critically damped</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. A critically damped solution has the form
              <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{-at} + c_2 t e^{-at}</m>, with a repeated
              exponential rate. Here we have two different rates, so the
              roots are distinct, not repeated.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Underdamped</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. An underdamped solution involves oscillating trig
              terms. This solution has no trig functions, so it decays
              without oscillating.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 2: Equilibrium crossing -->

    <task xml:id="act-never-stretched-equilibrium">
      <title>Does the Mass Cross the Equilibrium for <m>t > 0</m>?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does the mass ever cross the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m>
          for <m>t > 0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Set <m>x(t) = 0</m> and determine whether there are any solutions
          with <m>t > 0</m>. Since <m>x(0) = 0</m> we already know <m>t = 0</m>
          is one solution. We want to know if there are any others.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We solve <m>-2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t} = 0</m>.
          Factoring out <m>2e^{-2t}</m> (which is never zero):
          <me>2e^{-2t}(-e^{t} + 1) = 0 \implies e^{t} = 1 \implies t = 0.</me>
          The only solution is <m>t = 0</m>. The mass <alert>never crosses
          the equilibrium</alert> for <m>t > 0</m>. Note that the mass does
          slowly approach zero as <m>t \to \infty</m>, but it never actually
          reaches or crosses it again.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 3: Initial conditions checkpoint -->

    <task xml:id="act-never-stretched-initial">
      <title>Which Way Does the Mass Immediately Move?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          We are given <m>x(0) = 0</m> and <m>x'(0) = -2</m>. The mass starts
          exactly at the equilibrium position. Based on the initial velocity,
          does the spring immediately become compressed or stretched?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Compressed, because <m>x'(0) = -2 \lt 0</m> means the mass immediately moves in the negative direction.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Since <m>x'(0) \lt 0</m>, the mass moves in the negative
              direction right away, meaning <m>x(t) \lt 0</m> for small <m>t > 0</m>,
              which is the compressed side. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Stretched, because the mass starts at equilibrium and must move somewhere.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The initial velocity tells us which direction the mass moves.
              Since <m>x'(0) = -2 \lt 0</m>, it moves in the negative (compressed)
              direction, not the positive (stretched) direction.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>We cannot tell from the initial conditions alone.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              We can tell. The sign of <m>x'(0)</m> tells us the direction of
              motion immediately after <m>t = 0</m>. Since <m>x'(0) = -2 \lt 0</m>,
              the mass moves in the negative (compressed) direction.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE CHECKPOINT: GRAPH MATCHING
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now that we have worked through the activity, we have determined the following:
    <ul>
      <li><p>The system is <alert>overdamped</alert>: no oscillations, the mass decays smoothly toward zero.</p></li>
      <li><p>The mass <alert>starts at equilibrium</alert> (<m>x(0) = 0</m>) and <alert>immediately becomes compressed</alert> (<m>x'(0) = -2 \lt 0</m>).</p></li>
      <li><p>The mass <alert>never crosses the equilibrium</alert> for <m>t > 0</m>.</p></li>
    </ul>
    Using these three facts, match the graph of <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m> below.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-overdamped-never-stretched-matching">
    <caption>
      Three candidate graphs for <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m>.
      The dashed green line marks the equilibrium <m>x = 0</m>.
      The red dot marks the starting position at <m>t = 0</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-overdamped-never-stretched-matching"
      iframe="ch3-graph/static-overdamped-never-stretched-matching.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2">
      <description>
        Three side-by-side candidate graphs labeled (a), (b), and (c),
        each with a dashed green equilibrium line at x equals zero and
        a red dot marking the starting position at t equals zero, which
        is on the equilibrium line for all three graphs.
        Graph (a): starts at zero, immediately moves below zero into
        compressed territory, reaches a minimum, then turns around and
        crosses the equilibrium line, going above zero before eventually
        returning toward zero from above.
        Graph (b): starts at zero, immediately moves below zero into
        compressed territory, reaches a minimum, then increases back
        toward zero from below without ever crossing the equilibrium line.
        Graph (c): starts at zero and oscillates above and below the
        equilibrium line multiple times with decreasing amplitude,
        eventually approaching zero.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <task xml:id="task-never-stretched-matching">
    <title>Match the Graph</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph matches <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Graph (a)</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Graph (a) does start at zero and immediately becomes compressed,
            which is correct. However, it then crosses the equilibrium line
            and becomes stretched. We showed in the activity that the mass
            never crosses the equilibrium for <m>t > 0</m>, so this cannot
            be correct.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Graph (b)</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Graph (b) starts at zero, immediately becomes compressed,
            reaches a minimum, and then slowly approaches zero from below without
            ever crossing the equilibrium, exactly matching what we found
            in the activity. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Graph (c)</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Graph (c) shows oscillations, which is the behavior of an
            underdamped system. Since <m>x(t) = -2e^{-t} + 2e^{-2t}</m>
            has two distinct real exponential terms and no trig functions,
            the system is overdamped and does not oscillate.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </task>

  <p>
    Notice that in graph (b), the mass starts at zero, immediately becomes
    compressed, and then slowly approaches the equilibrium from below as
    <m>t \to \infty</m>. It gets closer and closer to zero but never actually
    crosses it again. Since the mass starts at <m>x = 0</m>, immediately moves
    into compressed territory, and never crosses the equilibrium, we can
    conclude the spring is <alert>never stretched</alert> for any <m>t > 0</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="video-overdamped-never-stretched">
    <caption>This video goes through the content of this page.</caption>
    <video youtube="cmy5o1ydrE8" width="100%" preview="default"/>
  </figure>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-4-3"
    iframe="question-form.html?section=3.4.3"
    width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</section>
